  "final_answer": "All three deliverables exist in `/app` and are internally consistent.\n\n**`fairhousing_audit.csv`** \u2014 header + 30 rows, two columns (`section_id`,`finding`), inventory order, labels joined with `|` in rule order (\u00a71 \u2192 \u00a72 \u2192 \u00a74). Nothing else in the file.\n\n**`fairhousing_memo.md`** \u2014 full reasoning, audited against RE-FH-9 as amended (\u00a75a adds `marital status` + `source of income` to the 11 required classes; \u00a75b brings `agent_bio` into \u00a74 scope).\n\n**`results.json`** \u2014 `section_count: 30, flagged_count: 22, personal_narrative_count: 7, protected_lang_count: 14, disclaimer_missing_count: 11`.\n\nHeadline for legal: the book is **not** printable as-is. The dominant issue is the standing disclosure paragraph being *abridged* under the amended standard \u2014 that strips its \u00a73 exemption (so its terms trip \u00a72) **and** makes responsible sections `DISCLAIMER_MISSING`. Two judgment calls worth legal's eye are flagged in \u00a74 of the memo: (1) `disclosure_block` is not a \u00a74-responsible role per the standard's literal text, so SEC-04/SEC-22 carry no `DISCLAIMER_MISSING`; (2) abridged notices are treated as carrying protected-class language, per \u00a73. Per-section rationale and a remediation checklist are in the memo.",
